{
  "$comment": "Vault item-type conformance cases (ADR 0087 section 8, ADR 0139). Both parsers and both native projections read this file: crates/vault-item-types (tests/conformance.rs) and packages/vault-item-types (src/conformance.test.ts). A definition valid on one plane must be valid on the other, a rejection on one a rejection on the other, and a projection must write and read back the same pass entry, or `opensesame pass` and the PWA disagree about what a stored item is. definitionCases: `set` is an ordered list of [JSON Pointer, value] pairs applied to a fresh copy of `draft` (a pointer may create a missing object key, and a final `-` appends to an array, as RFC 6901 spells it); `trust` is community unless it says platform; then exactly one of `codes` (every code must be refused with), `codesAnyOf` (at least one; used only where the host plane's serde refuses a closed enum as `syntax` and the TypeScript parser names the rule), or `valid: true`. projectionCases: with `values`, the type's projection must equal `entry` (and `rendered`, when given) and its readback is checked; without `values`, `entry` is the input read back. `readback.values` is exactly the fields read back (strings, lists, or record parts; the secret field always reads back, empty or not); `readback.extra` is exactly the unclaimed trailer keys kept.",
